WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.310
(projector buzzing)

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.790
- [Narrator] This is the world's largest

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:05.570
oil processing plant,

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:08.430
located in the Eastern
Province of Saudi Arabia.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:12.180
Early on Saturday, September
14th, a series of explosions

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.600
rocked the facility, and the
world woke to see it burning.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.240
- [Reporter] Satellite
images show thick black smoke

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.760
billowing from the Aramco
Oil Facility in Abqaiq.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.230
- [Reporter] The nation's
state-run oil company,

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.840
Saudi Aramco, has temporarily
closed its facilities,

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:30.340
which many experts expect
will cause a lack of supply

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.840
leading to higher global oil prices.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.900
- [Narrator] But while the
world was looking over here,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.120
energy traders on Wall
Street and elsewhere

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:42.750
were also looking over
here and here and here

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.350
at thousands of storage
sites around the world

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.633
where companies and
countries stockpile oil.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.740
What's inside these tanks

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.020
can be valuable information for traders

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.980
who make big bets based on
how much supply is available

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:56.780
and where in the world it's located.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.300
For traders, an event like
the one in Saudi Arabia

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.450
can be an opportunity.
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:06.010
- So in the event of, you
know, market crisis situations

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.440
or disaster events,
there's a natural increase

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.260
in volatility, and we do
see increased interest

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.720
from our client base and
from prospective clients.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.170
- [Narrator] Mario De La
Ossa is an energy specialist

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.680
at Orbital Insight, a
company that estimates

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.220
the tanks' contents
using satellite imagery,

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.490
artificial intelligence, and
a lot of complicated math.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:25.840
- I guess to say, well, we are quite proud

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.320
of our coverage levels,
which are on the order

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.010
of seeing about 80% of the
world's oil tanks every week.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.760
- [Narrator] Orbital is
at the vanguard of firms

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.250
that sell data to hedge
funds, energy companies,

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.860
and others who are looking to
anticipate oil price moves.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.350
To get their hands on the data,

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:42.830
the first thing Orbital had to do

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.830
was find as many of
these tanks as it could.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:47.630
Employees train computers to spot them

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.540
by feeding them image
after image of oil tanks.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.250
Once the computers got the picture,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.810
they scanned satellite imagery

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:57.130
and found more than 25,000
tanks around the world.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:00.090
Orbital is constantly
collecting images of each tank

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:03.500
which its computers then examine
to estimate their contents.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.040
The key to doing that lies in the shadows,

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:09.340
particularly those cast
upon the tops of the tanks.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.550
- [Mario] A floating roof
tank goes up and down

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:14.640
according to the volume of
oil that's inside of the tank,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.120
and that's done to prevent
a vapor space from forming

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.350
that would be very bad in
terms of explosion risks.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.530
- [Narrator] Orbital's computers
measure the shadows cast

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.860
on and around the area of each tank.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.530
The shadow cast to the outside

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.000
is used to determine
the size of each tank,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.730
which can range from 50,000
barrels to more than a million.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:35.600
The shadows cast inside the structure

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.750
is used to measure how much
oil is inside the tank.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:40.500
Generally, the bigger the shadow,

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.170
the less oil there is inside.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:44.270
- So we have that going for us,

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.780
that where the position of the roof is,

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:50.270
it will correspond to the volume
of oil inside of the tank,

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:53.440
but then to actually
calculate the specific volume,

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.380
this does become, you know, complex

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.400
in terms of aligning the exact
position of the satellite,

00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.630
the position of the sun

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:03.260
to adjust for the shadow
length that was cast.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:04.820
- [Narrator] Orbital
then adds up the volumes

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:06.540
and compares them over time,

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.990
noting when stockpiles grow and
shrink in different regions.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.170
The company is able to do this quickly,

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:15.860
beating official reports by
weeks and sometimes even months,

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.650
and they can offer insight
to parts of the world

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:20.460
where supply data are scarce or suspect.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.850
- So how much is there in,
let's say, Cushing, Oklahoma,

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.510
and that's fairly well-tracked.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:26.990
What's not fairly well-tracked,

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:28.330
and this is one of the big problems

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:31.620
for people on the marketplace,
is how much oil is in China,

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.780
because the Chinese don't
openly disclose that.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.372
Historically, 80% of the
market has just been very dark.

00:03:38.372 --> 00:03:39.900
(pleasant electronic music)

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.480
- [Narrator] Knowing how
much oil is out there

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:42.670
and where it's stockpiled

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:45.100
is useful to a range of businesses.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.610
Energy producers use it to
decide whether to drill wells.

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:51.450
Refiners that turn crude
into products like gasoline

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.840
and jet fuel wanna know when
to buy their main ingredient.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.240
Hedge funds and other speculators
simply wanna make money.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:03:59.780
These traders might act on clues

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:01.130
that stockpiles are dwindling

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:04.420
by buying oil now and selling
it later once other buyers

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:07.760
realize supplies are low
and the price goes up.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.600
Predicting changes in the availability

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.830
and thus the price of oil
can be incredibly lucrative.

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:14.830
Andy Hall, who advises Orbital,

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:16.010
is one of the most successful

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.670
and well-known oil
traders of recent decades.

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:21.210
He made a fortune buying
boatloads of cheap oil

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:24.370
before Saddam Hussein
invaded Kuwait in 1990,

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:26.110
which touched off the first Gulf War

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:28.520
and sent crude prices soaring.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.250
Traders like Hall are always looking

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:32.820
for ways to gauge oil supply.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.250
They count the rigs
that are drilling wells

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.790
and measure what's
passing through pipelines.

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:41.160
They train cameras on import
and export loading docks

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.490
and count tankers moving through waterways

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:47.500
like the Houston Ship Channel
and the Strait of Hormuz.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.850
Firms like Orbital have
taken this kind of detection

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:51.760
to a whole new level.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:53.730
Before the attacks on Saudi Arabia,

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.930
Orbital observed a lot
of shrinking shadows

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:58.040
on the tops of the kingdom's oil tanks,

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.290
suggesting that it was
building up its stockpiles,

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.010
but after the attacks,
the shadows began to grow,

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:05.210
which meant that Saudi Arabia

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:08.080
was tapping reserves to satisfy customers,

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:09.970
but the firm didn't just look there.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:12.250
It looked at China, where tanks were full,

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.820
and the U.S., which
were also well-stocked,

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:18.060
and in Japan and South
Korea, which were not,

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.480
and in India, where the
shadows were growing

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.040
as imports from Saudi Arabia dwindled.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.400
Meanwhile, back on Wall
Street and in Houston

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.775
and London and Singapore,
traders placed their bets.

00:05:29.775 --> 00:05:33.025
(dramatic piano music)

